+<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">One language, practical building blocks</p><h2>Build scripts. Services. AI workflows.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Automate local work</h3><p>Work with files, processes, JSON and collections. Write small scripts with functions, structs and explicit error handling.</p><a href="/examples/files/">Explore file operations</a></article><article><h3>Build applications</h3><p>Use HTTP and networking helpers, modules, async tasks and generators. Check the documented runtime limits before choosing a deployment.</p><a href="/examples/http/">Inspect an HTTP server</a></article><article><h3>Make AI work inspectable</h3><p>Identify requests, replay recorded responses and validate structured data. Add packages when you need agent orchestration.</p><a href="/examples/replay/">Run an offline AI call</a></article></div></section>
+<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">AI-native, with concrete mechanisms</p><h2>Repeat the request. Inspect the result.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Stable request identity</h3><p><code>ai_request_hash</code> derives a deterministic request identifier while excluding credentials. Compare requests without sending them to a model.</p><a href="/examples/request-hash/">Run the hash example</a></article><article><h3>Offline model replay</h3><p><code>ai_chat</code> can read recorded cassettes. Reproduce a response in tests without a live provider, then validate its data with <code>json_schema_validate</code>.</p><a href="/examples/replay/">Replay a response</a> · <a href="/examples/structured-output/">Validate a schema</a></article></div><p>Conventional languages already offer async functions, HTTP clients and testing libraries. Kujo adds AI request identity, replay, streaming callbacks and capability controls at the runtime level. These mechanisms support review; they do not guarantee correctness or isolation.</p><p>Choose Kujo for scripts and tools that benefit from those primitives. Keep Python or TypeScript when existing libraries, deployment requirements or team experience matter more. Independent Kujo tools can still inspect and evaluate those repositories.</p><details><summary>What belongs to which layer?</summary><ul><li><strong>Language:</strong> functions, modules, collections, structs, enums, matching, async and generators.</li><li><strong>Rust VM/runtime:</strong> execution, host helpers, AI primitives and capability-aware access.</li><li><strong>Core CLI:</strong> check, format, lint, tests, LSP and documentation generation.</li><li><strong>Separate packages:</strong> Kennel, Agents SDK, AI SDK and workflow tools have their own installation and versions.</li><li><strong>External systems:</strong> live model providers require configuration and credentials; optional hosts and integrations are not bundled language features.</li></ul></details></section>
+<section class="language-section" id="get-started"><p class="eyebrow">Your first successful program</p><h2>Install. Check. Run.</h2><p>For a runtime-only install on supported macOS, Linux and Windows systems with Node.js 18 or newer:</p><div class="sample-code"><pre tabindex="0"><code>npm install --global @kujolang/kujo-runtime@1.9.0
+kujo --version</code></pre><button class="sk-button" data-copy-code hidden>Copy code</button><span role="status" data-code-status></span></div><p>Save <code>print("Hello, Kujo!")</code> in <code>hello.kujo</code>:</p><div class="sample-code"><pre tabindex="0"><code>kujo check hello.kujo
+kujo run hello.kujo</code></pre><button class="sk-button" data-copy-code hidden>Copy code</button><span role="status" data-code-status></span></div><p>Expected output: <code>Hello, Kujo!</code>. <a href="/language/">Native installers, Windows guidance and troubleshooting</a>.</p></section>
+<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">Ecosystem · installed separately</p><h2>Start with the job you need done.</h2><p>These are independent projects. Tools that inspect repositories or run configured commands can support existing languages; SDK code itself runs in Kujo.</p><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Build an AI agent</h3><p>Use Agents SDK with AI SDK for Kujo agent programs.</p><a href="/ecosystem/agents-sdk/">Agents SDK</a></article><article><h3>Coordinate multi-step work</h3><p>Define repeatable work and inspect its state.</p><a href="/ecosystem/dispatch/">Dispatch</a></article><article><h3>Understand a repository</h3><p>Inspect existing code and assemble context for a change.</p><a href="/ecosystem/scout/">Scout</a> · <a href="/ecosystem/scent/">Scent</a></article><article><h3>Evaluate generated work</h3><p>Run configured checks and browser assertions for your project.</p><a href="/ecosystem/eval/">Eval</a> · <a href="/ecosystem/lens/">Lens</a></article><article><h3>Review a change</h3><p>Turn Git diffs and footprint into inspectable evidence.</p><a href="/ecosystem/patchbrief/">PatchBrief</a> · <a href="/ecosystem/changebucket/">ChangeBucket</a></article><article><h3>Prepare a release</h3><p>Evaluate repository readiness against explicit checks.</p><a href="/ecosystem/shipcheck/">ShipCheck</a></article></div><p><a href="/ecosystem/">Browse the full ecosystem</a></p><h3>From request to reviewable change</h3><ol class="workflow-stages"><li>Define scope</li><li>Retrieve context</li><li>Implement in a chosen environment</li><li>Run tests</li><li>Inspect change evidence</li><li>Assess readiness</li></ol><p>Composable stages, not an automatically integrated pipeline. <a href="/development-workflow/">Follow a reproducible repository workflow</a>.</p></section>
+<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence over promises</p><h2>Inspect what people build.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>This website</h3><p>Kujo SSG generates static HTML; SiteKit supplies the visual system.</p><a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujolang.ai/blob/main/build.kujo">Inspect the build source</a></article><article><h3>Applications and services</h3><p>Explore the Kujo-backed CMS, CRUD API and RAG showcases with their runtime and integration boundaries.</p><a href="/ecosystem/cms/">CMS</a> · <a href="/ecosystem/crud-api/">CRUD API</a> · <a href="/ecosystem/rag/">RAG</a></article><article><h3>Performance and comparisons</h3><p>Separate VM execution, startup and application workloads. Cross-language results remain unmeasured unless a reproducible report says otherwise.</p><a href="/performance/">Measurement methodology</a> · <a href="/compare/">Compare language approaches</a></article></div></section>
+<section class="language-section"><h2>Start where you are.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Learn the language</h3><p>Prerequisite: a terminal and a supported runtime. Scope: one script, then functions and data. First action: install Kujo and run Hello World.</p><a href="/language/">Start the language guide</a></article><article><h3>Build AI agents</h3><p>Prerequisite: Kujo and the SDK dependencies. Scope: a small agent with explicit tools. First action: follow the Agents SDK installation and offline examples.</p><a href="/ecosystem/agents-sdk/">Start with Agents SDK</a></article><article><h3>Improve an existing workflow</h3><p>Prerequisite: a Git repository and its test commands. Scope: inspect a change and record evidence. First action: run the local workflow exercise.</p><a href="/development-workflow/">Keep your language, improve the workflow</a></article></div></section>
